※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A field of a record may be a record, and the value of one is written where it
※ will live rather than made and then copied.
※
※ **That is not an economy but the only way.**  A record travels as its fields,
※ so a field that is itself several values has no one register to be made in --
※ which is why a record holding a record could be declared and not written for as
※ long as a literal made a value before anything bound it.  Written straight into
※ the place that will hold it, a nested field goes one offset further in and no
※ whole record is ever in a register at all.
※
※ **Reading one follows the offsets.**  `l.to.x` is the place of `l`, plus where
※ `to` lies, plus where `x` lies, and only the field is read.  A reference into
※ one is that address, so `&l.to.x` is an address like any other.
※
※ Compare: **C**, whose nested initializer this is, and where a structure holding
※ a structure has always been laid out by the same rule; **Rust**, **Go** and
※ **Zig**, the same; **ML**, whose records nest the same way with the layout left
※ to the implementation, which is what `offsets_of` is here.

type Point = x : u32 ; y : u32
type Line = from : Point ; to : Point

@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut u32):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1u32

fn span(of: &Line) → u32:
    of⌖.to.x + of⌖.to.y

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let l : mut Line = Line(.from ← Point(.x ← 1u32, .y ← 2u32),
                            .to ← Point(.x ← 3u32, .y ← 4u32))
    ※ a reference to a field of a field, written through
    bump(&l.to.x)
    ※ and the whole of it read behind a reference
    let reached : &Line = &l
    if l.from.x ≠ 1u32 ∨ l.from.y ≠ 2u32:
        1u6
    elif l.to.x ≠ 4u32 ∨ l.to.y ≠ 4u32:
        2u6
    elif span(reached) ≠ 8u32:
        3u6
    elif reached⌖.from.y ≠ 2u32:
        4u6
    else:
        0u6
